65625, 0.0274505615234375, -0.007293701171875, -0.0165557861328125, 0.002025604248046875, -0.0241241455078125, 0.0014438629150390625, -0.006710052490234375, 0.00818634033203125, 0.004558563232421875, 0.017364501953125, -0.0243072509765625, 0.016143798828125, 0.017730712890625, 0.0139617919921875, -0.0057373046875, -0.00942230224609375, 0.0057830810546875, -0.0088043212890625, 0.02154541015625, -0.0092620849609375, -0.01114654541015625, -0.006450653076171875, -0.033935546875, -0.00030159950256347656, -0.00933837890625, -0.00472259521484375, -0.0217742919921875, 0.027313232421875, -0.0014667510986328125, 0.0075225830078125, -0.024383544921875, -0.00872039794921875, 0.0181121826171875, 0.0104827880859375, 0.002460479736328125, -0.0102996826171875, -0.02459716796875, 0.00466156005859375, -0.018768310546875, 0.00039458274841308594, -0.011871337890625, -0.0279998779296875, 0.016937255859375, -0.04388427734375, 0.01190185546875, -0.0238800048828125, -0.0217437744140625, 0.0089874267578125, -0.01371002197265625, -0.006908416748046875, -0.005451202392578125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014autonomous systems that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank of past experiences, solutions, and feedback\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. For example, such agents may solve programming puzzles, storing both their code attempts and the feedback received, and then use this memory to inform future problem-solving. However, the reliability aspects of these methods, such as their consistency, robustness to task order, and susceptibility to failure modes like catastrophic forgetting or performance variance, have been critically overlooked. In this work, we conduct a comprehensive re-evaluation of two representative memory-based methods: (1) Episodic Memory Replay, where the agent reuses past solutions and feedback verbatim, and (2) Memory-Augmented Policy Learning, where the agent retrieves and integrates relevant memory entries into its decision-making process. These methods differ in how they leverage stored information\u2014either by direct reuse or by contextual integration.\n\nWe broaden the scope of evaluation along two axes: (1) including multiple runs to quantify variance, which captures the impact of stochasticity in environments and agent decision-making, and (2) randomly shuffling the tasks to investigate the effect of task order, reflecting the unpredictability of task sequences in real-world deployment scenarios. These axes were chosen because, in practical applications, agents often face non-deterministic environments and unpredictable task sequences, making it essential to assess both the stability of learning outcomes and the generality of improvement across different conditions.\n\nThrough these experiments, we make two observations that expose the fragility of current methods: First, agent evaluation exhibits substantial noise in complex environments and on multi-step tasks, with standard deviations in success rates reaching up to 20% across runs. This noise stems from both environmental randomness (e.g., stochastic task feedback) and the agent\u2019s own stochastic policies. Stacking a self-improving loop on top can further amplify this noise, leading to inconsistent outcomes across runs\u2014for instance, in some runs, agents may solve all tasks, while in others, they fail early and never recover. Second, the agent's improvement is highly dependent on task order. For example, prior works often adopt default orderings where simpler tasks precede harder ones, effectively creating an implicit curriculum; this acts as a hidden prerequisite for success, as reversing or randomizing the order can significantly degrade performance, sometimes reducing final success rates by half.\n\nTo better understand this fragility, we manually examine the agents' memory by reviewing the textual entries stored after each task, focusing on the completeness and accuracy of encoded solutions and feedback. Specifically, we assess whether key steps, error messages, or success criteria are faithfully recorded, and whether omissions or misrepresentations occur. We hypothesize that task and environment underspecification\u2014where agents lack sufficient detail about task requirements or environmental feedback\u2014contributes to this fragility. To validate this hypothesis, we operationalize additional information by providing detailed rubrics outlining explicit success criteria and richer environment feedback (e.g., explicit error messages or stepwise hints) alongside the agent\u2019s own notes, and measure the resulting change in task success rates and memory quality. While this added information partially closes the performance degradation observed in previous experiments, significant gaps still remain: for example, agents may still misinterpret ambiguous feedback, fail to retrieve relevant memory entries, or overfit to spurious correlations in the data, leading to persistent failures on certain task types.\n\nLooking ahead, our work advocates for more rigorous evaluation protocols for self-improving agents by reporting results across multiple runs and stress-testing them under challenging conditions, such as randomized task orders and noisy environments. We recommend adopting metrics such as variance in success rates, minimum/maximum performance across runs, and robustness to task order as standard benchmarks for reliability. Moreover, our findings on underspecification call for systems and interfaces that enable effective human oversight, such as interactive dashboards for memory inspection, tools for injecting clarifying feedback, or mechanisms for human-in-the-loop correction, as explored in recent work on human-AI collaboration (e.g., the OpenAI Evals framework or interactive annotation tools). These directions are crucial for preventing agents from failing in unforeseeable ways and for building more reliable self-improving systems."}}